# Cursor Rule: Next.js PWA Performance Optimization

## Purpose

These rules must be followed by the AI assistant for any performance-related changes, reviews, or suggestions in this Next.js PWA project. They are designed to maintain and improve high Lighthouse scores and real-world user experience.

---

## General Principles

- Prioritize performance, accessibility, best practices, and SEO in all code changes.
- Always explain the performance impact of your suggestions.
- Proactively identify and fix any code that could degrade performance.

---

## JavaScript & Main Thread

- Minimize main-thread work: avoid heavy computations in the UI thread.
- Use dynamic imports (`next/dynamic`) for non-critical or heavy components.
- Remove unused code and dependencies.
- Prefer lighter, tree-shakeable libraries over large third-party packages.
- Enable and keep `swcMinify: true` in `next.config.js`.
- Use Web Workers for heavy computations if needed.

## Images & Media

- Use the Next.js `<Image />` component for all images.
- Always set explicit `width` and `height` to prevent layout shifts (CLS).
- Use the `priority` prop for above-the-fold images (especially LCP).
- Serve modern formats (WebP, AVIF) and use responsive `sizes` attributes.
- Avoid large GIFs; use video or Lottie for animations.

## Fonts

- Use `next/font` for all custom fonts (Google or local).
- Always use `display: swap` to avoid invisible text during load.
- Limit the number of font weights/styles loaded.

## Code Splitting & Lazy Loading

- Use dynamic imports for client-only or rarely-used components.
- Use skeleton loaders for content that loads asynchronously.
- Only hydrate what’s necessary on the client.

## Caching & Data Fetching

- Use explicit Next.js caching strategies (`revalidate`, `tags`) for all fetches.
- Prefer static generation (SSG) for content that doesn’t change often.
- Use on-demand revalidation for dynamic content.
- Leverage browser cache for static assets.

## Service Worker & PWA

- Register the service worker in a dedicated client component, not in `layout.tsx`.
- Keep the service worker lean; avoid excessive caching of large or dynamic assets.
- Test offline mode and update strategies regularly.

## Back/Forward Cache (bfcache)

- Avoid using `unload` event listeners or patterns that block bfcache.
- Test bfcache using Chrome DevTools and Lighthouse.

## Scripts

- Use `<Script />` with the correct `strategy` (`afterInteractive`, `lazyOnload`, or `worker`) for third-party scripts.
- Only load critical scripts before interactive; defer analytics and chat widgets.

## Responsive & Mobile-First Design

- Use responsive CSS frameworks (e.g., Tailwind CSS) and test on all device sizes.
- Avoid fixed-width layouts and ensure touch targets are large enough.

## Monitoring & Continuous Improvement

- Use Vercel Analytics or similar to monitor real-user performance.
- Run Lighthouse audits on production builds regularly, not just in development.

